{
    // ── Fixed signal actions (D-03) ─────────────────────────────────────
    // waybar's default on-sigusr1 action alternates between shown and
    // hidden on every signal — safe only if exactly one sender ever
    // exists. Four actors now want to move the bar (idle watcher,
    // fullscreen watcher, gaming-mode, keybind), so a shared alternating
    // action desyncs the moment two of them disagree. Fixing on-sigusr1
    // to a constant `hide` kills that shared-alternating approach
    // structurally, at the config layer: no signal from any actor can
    // ever accidentally reveal a bar another actor just hid.
    //
    // Historically, the D-03 owner (bar-visibility.sh, renamed from its
    // pre-Phase-18 form) was the ONLY thing that decided show vs.
    // hide here, by choosing which fixed signal to send (SIGUSR1 = hide,
    // SIGUSR2 = reload/show). Phase 18 Plan 15 drops waybar's own
    // visibility control outright — the owner now actuates the QML bar
    // only, and waybar is left permanently visible for the remaining
    // waves (see that plan's "waybar Coexistence" section) — but this
    // fixed on-sigusr1 action stays as-is: reload.sh's own SIGUSR2 still
    // fires on every theme switch, and a fixed `hide` on-sigusr1 remains
    // the structurally-safe default until RETIRE-02 deletes this file.
    "on-sigusr1": "hide",

    // Already waybar's default — declared explicitly anyway so the
    // signal contract is visible at the config layer rather than merely
    // implied. `reload` is documented as resetting the bar to its
    // config-time visibility (i.e. mapped/visible), making it an
    // idempotent "show" the owner reuses for free — and it's already the
    // signal the theme pipeline sends on every theme switch
    // (theme-engine/lib/reload.sh), so this costs nothing new.
    "on-sigusr2": "reload"

    // Included by every config-{full,athena,floating,vertical}.jsonc's `include`
    // array. Under waybar's first-defined-wins semantics, no layout
    // defines these keys itself, so this file's values apply to all of
    // them uniformly — declared once, not four times (D-31's shared-
    // include pattern, extended to bar-level signal config).
}
